chess pieces — standard SVG set
===============================

Twelve files, `assets/chess/<colour>_<piece>.svg`, vendored for a chess game.

Source:  Wikimedia Commons, the "Cburnett" chess piece set.
         https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:SVG_chess_pieces
Author:  Cburnett (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:Cburnett)
Licence: CC BY-SA 3.0 — https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/
         Attribution is therefore required, and this file is it. Share-alike
         applies to modified versions of these images.

Each piece is drawn on a transparent 45×45 square, which is what the `t45`
suffix in the upstream filename means. Nothing has been modified except the
filenames; the SVG bytes are byte-for-byte as downloaded.

Filename mapping
----------------

The upstream names encode piece and colour in one letter each
(`Chess_<piece><colour>t45.svg`), which is compact but unreadable at a call
site. Local names spell both out.

    Chess_klt45.svg  ->  white_king.svg      Chess_kdt45.svg  ->  black_king.svg
    Chess_qlt45.svg  ->  white_queen.svg     Chess_qdt45.svg  ->  black_queen.svg
    Chess_rlt45.svg  ->  white_rook.svg      Chess_rdt45.svg  ->  black_rook.svg
    Chess_blt45.svg  ->  white_bishop.svg    Chess_bdt45.svg  ->  black_bishop.svg
    Chess_nlt45.svg  ->  white_knight.svg    Chess_ndt45.svg  ->  black_knight.svg
    Chess_plt45.svg  ->  white_pawn.svg      Chess_pdt45.svg  ->  black_pawn.svg

(`l` is light/white and `d` is dark/black. Piece letters are the usual
algebraic ones: k q r b n p.)

Fetched from `https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/<name>`, which
resolves to the current file bytes. Do not re-download at runtime: the game
renders from the vendored copies so a network hiccup or a deleted upstream file
cannot break play.

Why this set
------------

It is the de facto standard chess diagram set: unambiguous at small sizes,
drawn to a consistent 45px grid, and already the shape players recognise from
Wikipedia. The pieces are pure geometry with no board, so they can be drawn onto
a board of any colour — which a raster screenshot of a board could not.
